## Summary

The lines below use each work-precision set and its own dashed sample-error line. A method reaches that line when at least one measured weak error is at or below it; reaching methods are ordered by their cheapest qualifying elapsed time. The dashed "Sample Error: 1000" line comes from `DiffEqDevTools.get_sample_errors`, which currently divides by $\sqrt{N}$ twice, so it is a factor of about $\sqrt{1000}$ below $E_S = \sqrt{V/N}$ (a fix is proposed in https://github.com/SciML/OrdinaryDiffEq.jl/pull/4612); treat comparisons against that line as provisional.

```julia
println("Weak work-precision results computed from the curves above:")
for result in weak_results
    reaching = NamedTuple{(:name, :time),Tuple{String,Float64}}[]
    missing = String[]
    for method in result.methods
        qualifying_times = [time for (error, time) in zip(method.errors, method.times) if isfinite(error) && error <= result.line && isfinite(time) && time >= 0]
        if isempty(qualifying_times)
            push!(missing, method.name)
        else
            push!(reaching, (name=method.name, time=minimum(qualifying_times)))
        end
    end
    sort!(reaching; by=r -> r.time)
    ordered = isempty(reaching) ? "none" : join(["$(r.name) ($(round(r.time; sigdigits=3)) s)" for r in reaching], " < ")
    not_reaching = isempty(missing) ? "none" : join(missing, ", ")
    println("  $(result.problem) ($(result.set)), sample-error line = $(round(result.line; sigdigits=4)): reaching, cheapest first: $ordered; not reaching: $not_reaching")
end
```

